From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GPU] Let title code read GPU-written memory back into guest
 memory

Unless readback_memexport is on, what shaders write with memory export
never reaches guest memory, and readback_memexport waits for the GPU at
every export. Some games read such results on the CPU only at known
moments, after waiting for a fence themselves (Halo 3: the image of a
Theater screenshot, its effect storages when it saves the game state).

guest_readback.h (header-only, so title code can use it without linking
the GPU code): a guest thread names up to 8 physical ranges and blocks
while the command processor thread copies the bytes whose host GPU copy
is newer than guest memory into guest memory. Those bytes are
SharedMemory::ForEachGpuNewerPart: pages last written by the GPU with no
CPU write since, and elsewhere the memory export results
memexport_keep_gpu_data keeps. Right after a load, nothing.

The command processor thread polls the request with one relaxed atomic
load at the top of its loop, in its idle spin and in WAIT_REG_MEM's
poll, all after the packet the guest waited for, and takes it with
try_lock (it waits for the requester only to finish, for a few stores),
and the idle spin rechecks the ring, not the flag, before executing.
D3D12 and Vulkan open a submission, copy the parts to the readback
buffer, await all queue operations, then copy into guest memory through
the physical view (which has no write watches, so nothing is
invalidated) under the global lock, skipping any part the CPU wrote
since. A request the thread doesn't take in time is withdrawn and
changes nothing; a taken one always finishes before the requester
returns, so guest memory never changes behind the title's back (Halo 3
hashes its save right after). Requests from the command processor thread
itself (guest interrupt callbacks) are refused.

Meant for memory export targets: a resolve at a draw resolution scale
above 1 isn't in the shared memory copy, and the command processor
writes fences and occlusion query results to guest memory directly, so
ranges with those must not be requested.

Also: D3D12 refuses the copy if the submission couldn't be ended, and
Vulkan destroys a replaced readback buffer only once the submissions
that may copy to it have completed.

No cost unless a title asks: no cvar, nothing changes in IssueDraw, the
uploads or the per-frame paths.
